/*!
    \title Qt Graphs 2D Rendering Backends
    \page qtgraphs-backends-2D.html
    \brief Comparison of the two rendering backends available for 2D graphs.

    Qt Graphs for 2D can draw a \l GraphsView with either of two rendering
    backends:

    \list
        \li The \b {high-quality} backend, which draws with
            \l {Qt Quick Shapes}. This is the default.
        \li The \b {high-performance} backend, which draws with
            \l {Qt Canvas Painter}. This backend is available only if you
            enable it when you build the Qt Graphs module.
    \endlist

    Both backends produce the same graphs from the same API. They differ only
    in how they draw them.

    \note \l {Building with a Backend}{The build} determines which backends
    are available. Each graph then picks one of them
    \l {Selecting the Backend at Run Time}{at run time}.

    \section1 High-Quality Backend

    The high-quality backend draws each series with \l {Qt Quick Shapes},
    using \l{QtQuick.Shapes::Shape::preferredRendererType}{Shape.CurveRenderer},
    which keeps strokes and curved edges smooth at any scale. The shapes become
    part of the scene graph, so the graph is rendered without an intermediate
    render target.

    \section1 High-Performance Backend

    The high-performance backend draws the whole graph with
    \l {Qt Canvas Painter}. All of the 2D renderers paint into a single
    \l QCanvasPainterItem, a \l QQuickRhiItem that renders into an offscreen
    color buffer sized to the item, so the graph is one scene graph item no
    matter how many series it contains, and it is resampled rather than
    redrawn if the item is scaled by a transformation.

    \section1 Choosing a Backend

    The high-quality backend is the default, and is the right choice unless
    there is a specific reason to change.

    Neither backend redraws a graph that does not change, so for a graph that
    is drawn once and then left alone, the choice only affects visual quality.
    The difference in rendering cost shows up when the data, the axis ranges,
    or the size of the graph keep changing, because that is when the
    high-quality backend has to rebuild its shape geometry.

    The choice does not affect \l ScatterSeries. Neither backend draws its
    points. They are Qt Quick items, created from the component set as
    \l {XYSeries::pointDelegate}{pointDelegate}, or from a built-in marker
    when the series sets none. The other XY series draw their points the
    same way when they set a delegate. Their lines and areas still go
    through the backend.

    Consider the high-performance backend when:

    \list
        \li The graph contains a large number of series, or series with a
            large number of points.
        \li The data is updated continuously, for example from a live sensor
            feed, so the shape geometry would have to be rebuilt on every
            frame.
        \li The application runs on hardware where the number of scene graph
            nodes and draw calls is the limiting factor.
    \endlist

    Stay with the high-quality backend when:

    \list
        \li The graph is scaled, rotated, or otherwise transformed by its
            parent items, or is rendered into a
            \l {QtQuick::Item::layer.enabled}{layer} that is scaled.
        \li Visual fidelity of strokes and curves matters more than the cost
            of drawing them.
        \li You don't want to add a dependency on the
            \l {Qt Canvas Painter} module.
    \endlist

    \section1 Building with a Backend

    The following table describes the two configure features that control the
    backends.

    \table
        \header
            \li Feature
            \li Default
            \li Requires
        \row
            \li \c graphs-2d-high-quality-backend
            \li \c ON
            \li Nothing beyond the normal Qt Graphs dependencies.
        \row
            \li \c graphs-2d-high-performance-backend
            \li \c OFF
            \li The \l {Qt Canvas Painter} module (\c qtcanvaspainter).
    \endtable

    You can enable the high-performance backend only if Qt Canvas Painter is
    available. If you request the feature in a build without that module,
    configure fails with an error.

    To enable the high-performance backend when configuring Qt:

    \badcode
    ./configure -feature-graphs-2d-high-performance-backend
    \endcode

    To do the same with CMake:

    \badcode
    cmake -DFEATURE_graphs_2d_high_performance_backend=ON <source>
    \endcode

    To disable the high-quality backend, pass
    \c{-no-feature-graphs-2d-high-quality-backend} to \c configure, or
    \c{-DFEATURE_graphs_2d_high_quality_backend=OFF} to CMake.

    The \c {Qt Graphs 2D} section of the configure summary lists the resulting
    state as \c {High Quality} and \c {High Performance}.

    The two features are independent, and you can enable both. A graph then
    chooses between them
    \l {Selecting the Backend at Run Time}{at run time}.

    Axes, grids, \l BarSeries, \l ScatterSeries, and the delegates of
    \l CustomSeries do not go through either backend, so Qt Graphs draws
    them even in a build that has neither. \l AreaSeries, \l LineSeries,
    \l PieSeries, and \l SplineSeries do need one, and configuring a build
    that has those graph types but no backend fails with an error. To build
    the backend-less variant, disable those graph types as well:

    \badcode
    ./configure -no-feature-graphs-2d-high-quality-backend \
                -no-feature-graphs-2d-area \
                -no-feature-graphs-2d-donut-pie \
                -no-feature-graphs-2d-line \
                -no-feature-graphs-2d-spline
    \endcode

    \l {Qt Graphs Configure Options} describes how to enable only the graph
    types that you need.

    \section1 Selecting the Backend at Run Time

    The high-performance backend is off by default. Unless Qt Graphs was built
    with it enabled, there is nothing to select between, and the graph is
    always drawn with Qt Quick Shapes.

    In a build that has both backends, each \l GraphsView selects its backend
    with the \l {GraphsView::useCanvasPainter} property:

    \qml
    GraphsView {
        anchors.fill: parent

        // Draw this graph with the high-performance backend.
        useCanvasPainter: true

        LineSeries {
            XYPoint { x: 0; y: 0 }
            XYPoint { x: 1; y: 1 }
        }
    }
    \endqml

    The property defaults to \c false when the high-quality backend is
    available, and to \c true when the build has only the high-performance
    backend. In a build without the high-performance backend, setting the
    property to \c true has no effect and produces no warning.

    \section1 Extending the Backends

    A \l CustomSeries draws itself with its
    \l {CustomSeries::delegate}{delegate} component, which works regardless of
    which backend draws the rest of the graph.

    A custom series can also paint with \l QCanvasPainter, by passing a
    \l QCustomSeriesCanvasRenderer subclass to
    \l {QCustomSeries::setCustomSeriesPainter()}. That method only exists in a
    build that has the high-performance backend, and the painter is only used
    while that backend is drawing the graph.

    \sa {Qt Graphs Configure Options}, {Qt Graphs Overview for 2D},
        GraphsView, {Qt Canvas Painter}
*/
